		<profileDesc>
			<abstract>
				<p>We examine audience-specific how-to guides on wikiHow, in English, diachronically by comparing predictions from fine-tuned language models and human judgments. Using both early and revised versions, we quantitatively and qualitatively study how gender-specific features are identified over time. While language model performance remains relatively stable in terms of macro F 1-scores, we observe an increased reliance on stereotypical tokens. Notably, both models and human raters tend to overpredict women as an audience, raising questions about bias in the evaluation of educational systems and resources.</p>
			</abstract>
